2025-09-05 00:08:38,792 - evaluation_logger_Cross-176 - INFO - Final Response: Here are great attractions within easy reach of Hotel Route-Inn Sakaide-Kita Inter (Sakaide/Takamatsu area), with what to expect, highlights, and practical tips. Drive times are approximate from the hotel.

- Seto Ohashi Memorial Park and Commemorative Hall (Sakaide)
  - Why go: Best close-up views of the Seto Ohashi Bridge at the Shikoku end. Wide lawns, seaside promenade, playgrounds, and a free museum explaining how the bridge was built.
  - Highlights: Sunset photos of the bridge, scale models and interactive exhibits in the Commemorative Hall, seasonal flowers.
  - Time needed: 1–2 hours.
  - Access: 10–15 min by car. Museum typically 9:00–17:00, closed Mon; park open daily. Free park; museum usually free.

- Shikoku Aquarium (Utazu)
  - Why go: A modern aquarium focused on Seto Inland Sea ecosystems, with large tanks, jellyfish displays, and dolphin/sea lion presentations.
  - Highlights: “Kuroshio”-style main tank, outdoor pools with Seto Ohashi backdrop, kid-friendly touch areas.
  - Time needed: 1.5–2.5 hours.
  - Access: 10–15 min by car. Open daily with seasonal hours (often around 9:00–18:00). Adult admission roughly 2,200–2,400 JPY; verify current pricing.

- Ritsurin Garden (Takamatsu)
  - Why go: One of Japan’s finest daimyo strolling gardens, 400 years old, with sculpted pines, koi-filled ponds, and teahouses framed by Mt. Shiun.
  - Highlights: Wasen (traditional boat) ride, matcha at Kikugetsu-tei, early-morning light and reflections, seasonal blooms.
  - Time needed: 1.5–2.5 hours.
  - Access: 30–35 min by car. Admission around 410 JPY; opening hours vary seasonally (early opening in summer).

- Takamatsu Castle (Tamamo Park)
  - Why go: A rare seaside castle with seawater moats; tranquil grounds in the city center near the port.
  - Highlights: Restored turrets and gates, garden paths along the moats, spring cherry blossoms.
  - Time needed: 45–90 min.
  - Access: 30–35 min by car. Small admission fee; hours vary by season.

- Sunport Takamatsu and Symbol Tower
  - Why go: Waterfront district for dining, shopping, and Seto Inland Sea views; gateway to ferries for the art islands. The Symbol Tower offers observation decks and city views.
  - Highlights: Sunset promenade, the Red Lighthouse walkway, port-side eateries.
  - Time needed: 1–2 hours, longer if shopping or dining.
  - Access: About 30–35 min by car. Tool data: Takamatsu Symbol Tower is roughly 12 km from the hotel and averages 8.1/10 from 15 votes.

- Yashima Plateau and Yashima-ji Temple (#84 of the Shikoku 88)
  - Why go: A distinctive mesa overlooking the island-dotted Seto Inland Sea; historic site of the 12th‑century Battle of Yashima.
  - Highlights: Panoramic viewpoints, Yashima-ji temple precincts, optional visit to the new Yashima Aquarium on the summit.
  - Time needed: 1.5–3 hours.
  - Access: 40–50 min by car via the scenic summit road. Some paid parking near the top.

- Shikoku Mura (Shikoku Village) and Shikoku Mura Gallery
  - Why go: Open-air museum of traditional Shikoku architecture set on a wooded hillside; adjacent contemporary art gallery designed by Tadao Ando.
  - Highlights: Thatched farmhouses, suspension vine bridge replica, old lighthouse keeper’s house; tranquil trails.
  - Time needed: 1.5–2.5 hours.
  - Access: 40–45 min by car (at the base of Yashima). Admission charged; combination tickets available.

- Marugame Castle
  - Why go: One of Japan’s 12 original wooden castle keeps, perched atop impressive stone walls with commanding views.
  - Highlights: Steep ishigaki walls, compact tenshu, seasonal flowers.
  - Time needed: 60–90 min.
  - Access: 20–25 min by car. Small admission fee for the keep; hillside paths can be steep.

- Kotohira-gu Shrine (Konpira-san), Kotohira
  - Why go: Iconic Shinto shrine devoted to seafaring; lively temple town atmosphere along the approach.
  - Highlights: 785 steps to the main hall (1,368 to the inner shrine), mountain views, nearby Kanamaruza—Japan’s oldest wooden kabuki theater.
  - Time needed: 2–4 hours.
  - Access: 35–40 min by car. Bring water and good shoes; shops line the approach.

- Naoshima or Teshima (Setouchi Art Islands) – day trip
  - Why go: World-class contemporary art set amid rustic island life and Seto Inland Sea views.
  - Highlights: Naoshima’s Chichu Art Museum, Benesse House, Art House Project; Teshima’s ethereal Teshima Art Museum and village installations.
  - Time needed: Full day.
  - Access: 30–35 min by car to Takamatsu Port, then ferry (Naoshima ~50–60 min; Teshima ~35–45 min). Many museums close on certain weekdays; reserve timed tickets in advance (especially Chichu and Teshima Art Museum).

- Bonus (quiet local gem): Higashiyama Kaii Setouchi Art Museum (Sakaide)
  - Why go: Small museum dedicated to painter Kaii Higashiyama, with serene works and windows framing the Seto Inland Sea.
  - Time needed: 45–60 min.
  - Access: About 10–15 min by car. Modest admission; limited hours.

Tips
- Driving is the most convenient from your hotel’s interchange location. Parking is generally easy outside central Takamatsu.
- If using trains, JR Sakaide connects to Takamatsu Station in about 20–30 minutes; local buses and Kotoden lines then reach sights like Ritsurin Garden.
- Opening hours, closures, and prices change seasonally—check official sites before you go, especially for museums and island ferries.

If you’d like, tell me your interests (gardens, history, art islands, food) and how much time you have; I can tailor a half-day or full-day route from the hotel.

